.policy "python-practice-retry-without-limit" revision 1
.description "A retry must give up: pass tenacity a stop=, backoff a max_tries= or max_time=, and bound a hand-written retry loop."
.language python
.mode log
.scope include "**/*.py"
// Rerail's own rule, not a port. A retry that never gives up turns a permanent failure (bad credentials, a 4xx, a
// deleted row, a schema error) into an endless loop: a hung request, a stuck worker, a hot loop hammering the
// dependency. Outside test code it reports:
//   - tenacity (TenacityWithoutStop): `@retry`, `@retry(...)`, `retry(...)(f)`, `Retrying(...)` and
//     `AsyncRetrying(...)`, names resolved through imports (`tenacity.retry`, `from tenacity import retry as r`),
//     with no `stop=`. Tenacity's default is `stop_never` with `wait_none`: it retries forever, without waiting, on
//     any exception. A `retry=` predicate alone does not bound it: it picks which errors to retry, not how often.
//   - backoff (BackoffWithoutLimit): `@backoff.on_exception(...)` and `@backoff.on_predicate(...)` with neither
//     `max_tries=` nor `max_time=` (`max_value=` caps the wait, not the attempts).
//   - a hand-written loop (RetryLoopWithoutLimit): `while True:` (or `while 1:`) with a `try` among its statements
//     that leaves the loop once its work is done (a `return`, or a `break` of this loop, as a statement of the try
//     body or its `else`, or of a `with` there), one of whose `except` clauses never raises, returns, breaks this
//     loop or calls sys.exit()/os._exit(), and nothing else in the loop (a statement before or after the try, its
//     `finally`) does either: no counter or deadline the loop checks. A nested def or lambda is its own frame: a
//     raise there does not end the loop.
//   @retry(wait=wait_exponential(max=10))                 reported: no stop
//   @retry(stop=stop_after_attempt(3))                     not reported
//   @backoff.on_exception(backoff.expo, IOError)           reported
//   @backoff.on_exception(backoff.expo, IOError, max_time=60)   not reported
//   while True:                                            reported
//       try: conn = connect(); break
//       except OSError: time.sleep(1)
//   while True:                                            not reported: the handler gives up
//       try: return fetch()
//       except OSError:
//           attempt += 1
//           if attempt > 5: raise
// It leaves alone a `stop` (any value, also `stop_never`, which says the forever is meant) or `max_tries`/`max_time`
// (also None or a callable), keywords passed through `**kwargs` (they may hold the limit), Retrying's positional
// form (`Retrying(sleep, stop)`), a `for attempt in range(n)` loop, a `while True` worker or polling loop whose only
// exits are conditional (`if msg is None: break`, `if stop.is_set(): break`) or that has none (it is meant to run
// until told to stop), a loop whose looping handler catches only a wait's timeout (`except queue.Empty`, a redis
// `LockError` of `blocking_timeout`: it waits, it does not retry a failure), a handler of a name-conflict error
// (`EntityExistsError`, `IntegrityError`, ...) that picks a new value the try reads (a search for a free name), a loop
// that prompts the person at the terminal each turn (`input()`, `getpass()`, `click.prompt()`, `typer.prompt()`,
// rich's `Prompt.ask()`), a `while` with a real condition, `retry` names of other libraries (urllib3's Retry,
// celery's `self.retry`, a function of the run called retry), and test files.
// Gaps: a loop whose bound sits in a helper the handler calls (`self._check_attempts()` that raises) is reported; a
// poll-until-ready loop (`try: r = get(); if r.ok: break`) is not read, nor a loop whose try is nested in an `if` or
// `with` of the loop body; the `retrying` and `retry` packages (also unbounded by default) are not read; a tenacity
// `.retry_with(stop=...)` at the call site is not followed.

#include "lib/python_practice.rerail"
#include "python/exceptions.rerail"

.violation TenacityWithoutStop(at: node, what: symbol)
    "`{what}` has no `stop=`, and tenacity then retries forever (without waiting, unless `wait=` is set): a permanent error such as bad credentials or a 4xx becomes an endless loop. Add a stop, e.g. `stop=stop_after_attempt(5)` or `stop_after_delay(30)`; keep the retry. If it must retry until it succeeds, pass `stop=stop_never` to say so."
TenacityWithoutStop(e, what) :- prl_tenacity(e, what), pp_live(e).
.violation BackoffWithoutLimit(at: node, what: symbol)
    "`{what}` sets neither `max_tries=` nor `max_time=`, so backoff retries forever: a permanent error such as bad credentials or a 4xx becomes an endless loop. Add `max_tries=` or `max_time=`; keep the retry. If it must retry until it succeeds, pass `max_tries=None` to say so."
BackoffWithoutLimit(c, what) :- prl_backoff(c, what), pp_live(c).
.violation RetryLoopWithoutLimit(at: node, what: symbol, x: node)
    "This `while True` loop retries until its `try` succeeds, and `{what}` at line {x.line} neither raises, returns nor breaks, nor does anything else in the loop: a permanent error such as bad credentials or a 4xx loops forever. Count the attempts or set a deadline and re-raise once exceeded (or use tenacity with a `stop=`); leave it if the loop must run until it succeeds, such as a long-lived background connection."
RetryLoopWithoutLimit(w, what, x) :- prl_loop(w), x = min y : { prl_loop_handler(w, y) }, prl_what(x, what).

// ---- tenacity ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.decl prl_tenacity_q(q: symbol, what: symbol)
prl_tenacity_q("tenacity.retry", "retry"). prl_tenacity_q("tenacity.Retrying", "Retrying").
prl_tenacity_q("tenacity.AsyncRetrying", "AsyncRetrying").
prl_tenacity_q("tenacity.asyncio.AsyncRetrying", "AsyncRetrying").
// prl_tenacity(e, what): a call of retry/Retrying/AsyncRetrying with no stop, or a bare `@retry` decorator.
.decl prl_tenacity(e: node, what: symbol)
prl_tenacity(c, cat(p, cat(what, "(...)"))) :- kind(c, "call"), field(c, "function", f), sym_of(f, q),
    prl_tenacity_q(q, what), !kwarg(c, "stop", _), !pp_splat(c), !exc_arg(c, 1, _), prl_at(c, p).
prl_tenacity(m, "@retry") :- decorator(_, e), unparen(e, m), !kind(m, "call"), sym_of(m, "tenacity.retry").
// prl_at(c, p): p is "@" when call c is a decorator, else "".
.decl prl_deco(c: node)
prl_deco(c) :- decorator(_, e), unparen(e, c), kind(c, "call").
.decl prl_at(c: node, p: symbol)
prl_at(c, "@") :- prl_deco(c).
prl_at(c, "") :- kind(c, "call"), !prl_deco(c).

// ---- backoff -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.decl prl_backoff(c: node, what: symbol)
prl_backoff(c, cat(p, cat(cat("backoff.", m), "(...)"))) :- kind(c, "call"), field(c, "function", f), sym_of(f, q),
    (m = "on_exception" ; m = "on_predicate"), q = cat("backoff.", m), !kwarg(c, "max_tries", _),
    !kwarg(c, "max_time", _), !pp_splat(c), prl_at(c, p).

// ---- hand-written loops ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// prl_forever(w): `while True:` or `while 1:`.
.decl prl_forever(w: node)
prl_forever(w) :- kind(w, "while_statement"), field(w, "condition", c0), unparen(c0, c),
    (kind(c, "true") ; kind(c, "integer"), text(c, "1")).
// prl_loop_try(w, t): try t is a statement of forever-loop w's body.
.decl prl_loop_try(w: node, t: node)
prl_loop_try(w, t) :- prl_forever(w), field(w, "body", b), child(b, t, _), kind(t, "try_statement"), pp_live(w).
// prl_loopish(k): the nodes whose body a `break` cannot leave: loops and frames.
.decl prl_loopish(k: symbol)
prl_loopish("while_statement"). prl_loopish("for_statement"). prl_loopish("function_definition").
prl_loopish("lambda"). prl_loopish("class_definition").
// prl_exit(e, w): e, in loop w's own frame, leaves w: a return, a raise, a break whose loop is w, or a call of
// sys.exit / os._exit / exit / quit.
.decl prl_exit(e: node, w: node)
prl_exit(e, w) :- prl_forever(w), (kind(e, "return_statement") ; kind(e, "raise_statement")),
    within_stop(e, w, pp_stop).
prl_exit(e, w) :- prl_forever(w), kind(e, "break_statement"), nearest(e, w, prl_loopish).
prl_exit(e, w) :- prl_forever(w), kind(e, "call"), within_stop(e, w, pp_stop), field(e, "function", f), sym_of(f, q),
    (q = "sys.exit" ; q = "os._exit" ; q = "builtins.exit" ; q = "builtins.quit").
// prl_success(t, w): try t leaves w once its work is done: a `return` or a `break` of w that is a statement of the
// try body or its else clause, or of a `with` there, so it runs whenever nothing raised. A conditional exit
// (`if msg is None: break`, `if stop.is_set(): break`) is a worker's stop signal, not a retry's success.
.decl prl_success(t: node, w: node)
prl_success(t, w) :- prl_loop_try(w, t), field(t, "body", tb), prl_straight(tb, e), prl_leave(e, w).
prl_success(t, w) :- prl_loop_try(w, t), child(t, ec, _), kind(ec, "else_clause"), field(ec, "body", eb),
    prl_straight(eb, e), prl_leave(e, w).
.decl prl_leave(e: node, w: node)
prl_leave(e, w) :- prl_exit(e, w), (kind(e, "return_statement") ; kind(e, "break_statement")).
// prl_straight(b, e): statement e runs whenever block b runs to its end: a statement of b, or of a `with` body in b.
.decl prl_straight(b: node, e: node)
prl_straight(b, e) :- prl_try_block(b), child(b, e, _).
prl_straight(b, e) :- prl_straight(b, s), kind(s, "with_statement"), field(s, "body", wb), child(wb, e, _).
.decl prl_try_block(b: node)
prl_try_block(tb) :- prl_loop_try(_, t), field(t, "body", tb).
prl_try_block(eb) :- prl_loop_try(_, t), child(t, ec, _), kind(ec, "else_clause"), field(ec, "body", eb).
// prl_bounded(w, t): something in w outside try t (or in t's finally) leaves w: the counter or deadline check.
.decl prl_bounded(w: node, t: node)
prl_bounded(w, t) :- prl_loop_try(w, t), prl_exit(e, w), !within(e, t).
prl_bounded(w, t) :- prl_loop_try(w, t), child(t, fc, _), kind(fc, "finally_clause"), prl_exit(e, w), within(e, fc).
// An interactive prompt in the loop (`name = input(...)`, `click.prompt(...)`) asks again for new input: each turn
// is a new attempt the person at the terminal ends, not the same call repeated.
prl_bounded(w, t) :- prl_loop_try(w, t), kind(c, "call"), within_stop(c, w, pp_stop), field(c, "function", f),
    sym_of(f, q), prl_prompt_q(q).
.decl prl_prompt_q(q: symbol)
prl_prompt_q("builtins.input"). prl_prompt_q("getpass.getpass"). prl_prompt_q("click.prompt").
prl_prompt_q("click.confirm"). prl_prompt_q("typer.prompt"). prl_prompt_q("typer.confirm").
prl_prompt_q("rich.prompt.Prompt.ask"). prl_prompt_q("rich.prompt.Confirm.ask").
prl_prompt_q("rich.prompt.IntPrompt.ask").
// prl_handler_exits(x, w): handler x has a path that leaves w.
.decl prl_handler_exits(x: node, w: node)
prl_handler_exits(x, w) :- prl_loop_try(w, t), exc_handler(t, x), prl_exit(e, w), within(e, x).
// prl_loop_handler(w, x): handler x of a try in w retries: the try leaves w on success, x never leaves w, and
// nothing else in w bounds it. A handler that catches only the timeout of a wait (prl_wait_type) waits, it does not
// retry a failure: a consumer's `q.get(timeout=1)` then `except queue.Empty: continue`, or a redis lock taken with
// `blocking_timeout` then `except LockError: ...`; every other error still leaves the loop. A handler of a
// name-conflict error that picks a new value the try reads (`except EntityExistsError: name = f"{base}-{rand()}"`)
// searches for a free name: each turn sends something new.
.decl prl_loop_handler(w: node, x: node)
prl_loop_handler(w, x) :- prl_loop_try(w, t), prl_success(t, w), !prl_bounded(w, t), exc_handler(t, x),
    !prl_handler_exits(x, w), !prl_wait_only(x), !prl_new_name(x, t).
.decl prl_new_name(x: node, t: node)
prl_new_name(x, t) :- prl_loop_try(_, t), exc_handler(t, x), prl_conflict(x), exc_body(x, b), kind(a, "assignment"),
    within(a, b), field(a, "left", l), prl_target_name(l, v), field(t, "body", tb), kind(r, "identifier"),
    within(r, tb), text(r, v).
.decl prl_conflict(x: node)
prl_conflict(x) :- exc_type(x, e), text(e, s), (contains("Exist", s) ; contains("Duplicate", s) ;
    contains("Integrity", s) ; contains("Conflict", s) ; contains("Unique", s)).
// prl_target_name(l, v): assignment target l rebinds or updates the variable v: `v = ...`, `v[k] = ...`.
.decl prl_target_name(l: node, v: symbol)
prl_target_name(l, v) :- kind(l, "identifier"), text(l, v).
prl_target_name(l, v) :- kind(l, "subscript"), field(l, "value", o), kind(o, "identifier"), text(o, v).
.decl prl_wait_type(e: node)
prl_wait_type(e) :- exc_type(_, e), exc_qual(e, q),
    (q = "queue.Empty" ; q = "_queue.Empty" ; q = "asyncio.QueueEmpty" ; q = "multiprocessing.queues.Empty" ;
    q = "redis.exceptions.LockError").
.decl prl_other_type(x: node)
prl_other_type(x) :- exc_type(x, e), !prl_wait_type(e).
.decl prl_wait_only(x: node)
prl_wait_only(x) :- exc_type(x, _), !prl_other_type(x).
.decl prl_loop(w: node)
prl_loop(w) :- prl_loop_handler(w, _).
.decl prl_what(x: node, what: symbol)
prl_what(x, "except:") :- kind(x, "except_clause"), !field(x, "value", _).
prl_what(x, cat("except ", s)) :- field(x, "value", v), except_type(v, t), text(t, s).

// The cases, `cases/python-practice-retry-without-limit/`, read as one repository: each line ending in `# caught`
// is reported, and no other line.
.test cases exactly
    .fixture "app/__init__.py" from "../cases/python-practice-retry-without-limit/app/__init__.py"
    .fixture "app/backoffs.py" from "../cases/python-practice-retry-without-limit/app/backoffs.py"
    .fixture "app/clients.py" from "../cases/python-practice-retry-without-limit/app/clients.py"
    .fixture "app/consumers.py" from "../cases/python-practice-retry-without-limit/app/consumers.py"
    .fixture "app/loops.py" from "../cases/python-practice-retry-without-limit/app/loops.py"
    .fixture "app/names.py" from "../cases/python-practice-retry-without-limit/app/names.py"
    .fixture "app/other_retries.py" from "../cases/python-practice-retry-without-limit/app/other_retries.py"
    .fixture "app/prompts.py" from "../cases/python-practice-retry-without-limit/app/prompts.py"
    .fixture "app/settings.py" from "../cases/python-practice-retry-without-limit/app/settings.py"
    .fixture "tests/__init__.py" from "../cases/python-practice-retry-without-limit/tests/__init__.py"
    .fixture "tests/test_clients.py" from "../cases/python-practice-retry-without-limit/tests/test_clients.py"
    .expect _ at "app/backoffs.py":8
    .expect _ at "app/backoffs.py":13
    .expect _ at "app/backoffs.py":18
    .expect _ at "app/backoffs.py":27
    .expect _ at "app/consumers.py":27
    .expect _ at "app/clients.py":14
    .expect _ at "app/clients.py":19
    .expect _ at "app/clients.py":24
    .expect _ at "app/clients.py":29
    .expect _ at "app/clients.py":34
    .expect _ at "app/clients.py":40
    .expect _ at "app/clients.py":46
    .expect _ at "app/clients.py":51
    .expect _ at "app/clients.py":57
    .expect _ at "app/loops.py":14
    .expect _ at "app/loops.py":25
    .expect _ at "app/loops.py":34
    .expect _ at "app/loops.py":45
    .expect _ at "app/loops.py":55
    .expect _ at "app/loops.py":65
    .expect _ at "app/loops.py":214
    .expect _ at "app/names.py":36
    .expect _ at "app/prompts.py":30
.end
